      "narration": "Here is the problem you will absolutely hit, so let us prevent it rather than let you discover it. Your script works perfectly when you run it, and does nothing at all from cron. The cause is essentially always the environment. Cron does not run your shell configuration — no bashrc, no aliases, no virtual environment activation. It gives the job a minimal PATH containing usually just usr bin and bin, so any command installed anywhere else is simply not found. The working directory is your home, not wherever the script lives, so relative paths point somewhere unexpected. This is the non-interactive shell behaviour we covered in Part Four, and now you see why it mattered. The fixes are straightforward. Use absolute paths for everything, including the interpreter — write the full path to python, not just python. Do not depend on PATH at all. And critically, redirect both output and errors to a log file on every cron line, because otherwise cron mails the output to a local mailbox nobody reads, and your job fails silently for weeks. If you are still stuck, the debugging trick at the bottom is excellent: schedule a job that just dumps the environment to a file, look at it, and you will see exactly what your script is actually running with.",
